{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 100,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe began in a state of ultimate\nheat and compression in what we call the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5740,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it will “end” by expanding forever\ntowards a state of perfect cold and emptiness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11000,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s incredible that we could figure this\nmuch out, but we should not get too cocky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15300,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- big questions remain open."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18070,
    "dDurationMs": 2510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, what happened before the big bang?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20580,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What, if anything, happens after our universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23670,
    "dDurationMs": 7690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m about to give you the most outrageous\nhypothesis so far that may actually be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36200,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conformal Cyclic Cosmology is a story of the\norigin and the end of our universe from great"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42780,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical physicist Sir Roger Penrose."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45480,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s goes like this: the infinitely far\nfuture, when the universe has expanded exponentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50940,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to to an unthinkably large size, and every\nblack hole and particle has decayed into faint"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55949,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation .... that infinite stretch of space\nand time is identically the SAME THING as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62499,
    "dDurationMs": 6601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the infinitesimal and instantaneous big bang\nof a new universe, and our universe is just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69100,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one in an endless chain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71329,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know this is an outrageous proposal because\nRoger Penrose himself called it that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76469,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And as with all outrageous proposals it’s\nprobably wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80179,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if there’s a faint chance that it’s\nright it’s so bizarre that we should definitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85920,
    "dDurationMs": 1339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87259,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so, conformal cyclic cosmology, CCC."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91299,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here, conformal is for the “conformal scaling”\nneeded to turn your gigantic end of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97350,
    "dDurationMs": 2049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a tiny new big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99399,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A conformal transformation is just some mathematical\nfunction that you apply to a geometric space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104340,
    "dDurationMs": 3349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which preserves all of the angles in that\nspace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107689,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An example would be if you had a sheet of\nrubber and drew some lines on it, then expanded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113429,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rubber evenly in all directions - the\nlines would get longer and further apart,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118110,
    "dDurationMs": 3249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the angles at their intersections would\nstay the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121359,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is perhaps the simplest conformal transformation\n- just multiplying or dividing all dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126630,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the same scaling factor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128690,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We would say that our universe has conformal\ninvariance under scale changes - so the angles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134250,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t change if you change the size smoothly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136590,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But other things sure do change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137870,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It makes a big difference whether every atom\nin the universe is right next to each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141260,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or a billion light years apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143220,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let me try to give you a sense of a situation\nwhere scale might not matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147560,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A conformal scaling of spacetime means scaling\nboth space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151580,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, consider a universe that’s\none light-second across, and it exists for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156650,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the span of a single second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159010,
    "dDurationMs": 3130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light has time to travel across it once."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162140,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scale it up by around 30 quintillion times\nto describe a second universe that’s a billion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169010,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light years across and lasts a billion years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171780,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, light crosses it once in that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174960,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say these universes contain no matter\n- only photons - light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179620,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They both contain the same number of light\nrays, which begin traveling in the same direction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184079,
    "dDurationMs": 4101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although obviously they’re packed much closely\ntogether in the smaller universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188180,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the life of both universes, those rays\ntrace out the same pattern - all the angles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192750,
    "dDurationMs": 6709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between them stay the same, and the rays pass\nclose to each other the same number of times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199459,
    "dDurationMs": 3471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now these clearly aren’t the same thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202930,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light takes 30 quintillion times longer to\ncross one than the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206230,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But who’s to say that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208550,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember, the universes contain only light\n- no observers and no clocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213430,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s the key point: light does not\nexperience the flow of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219200,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those photons, the beginning of their\njourney is the same as their end, and these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222940,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes are equivalent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224680,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s a crude notion of how a tiny\nearly universe could be equivalent to a gigantic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231440,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "late universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232440,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was pretty loose compared to the formal\nexplanation of conformal cyclic cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237790,
    "dDurationMs": 1690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More “street” cyclic cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239480,
    "dDurationMs": 6899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s get a bit more rigorous - we’ll\ncall it business casual cyclic cosmology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246379,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To really compare the sizes of two chunks\nof spacetime we need to grid them up with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250599,
    "dDurationMs": 1151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rulers and clocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251750,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely, then, the big universe will have more\nlength and time ticks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255870,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll simplify things by gridding up an\nimaginary universe with only one dimension"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260530,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space on the x-axis and one dimension of\ntime on the y, and we choose our axes so that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266400,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light travels at a 45 degree path - the graph\nspans either 1 second in time and 1 light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272060,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "second in distance, or a billion years and\na billion lightyears, depending which universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276730,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re talking about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277770,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either way, light travels a 45 degree path."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281190,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a spacetime diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282970,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take two instantaneous events in this\nuniverse, separated in both space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288420,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The separation between them can\nbe determined by the number of gridlines of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291490,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time you pass on your journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294780,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now in Einstein’s universe it’s not quite\nthat simple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297680,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lines representing constant distance or simultaneous\ntimes shift with the velocity of the observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303470,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If I draw the line of constant time for all\npossible travelers passing by my position,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308260,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I get these nested curves - hyperbolas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312030,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They show how time will tick for any constant-velocity\nobserver passing through this point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317410,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, our episode on the geometry of\ncausality goes into all of this in more detail."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323470,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best way to define the separation between\ntwo events in spacetime is by the travel time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328160,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of something taking the most direct path between\nthem - a path of constant velocity that reaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333260,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that point in space at the right instant in\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336310,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the so-called spacetime interval,\nand it’s equal to the amount of time that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340870,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passes on the clock of the traveler - or the\nproper time of the traveler."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345110,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the number of these hyperbolic intervals\ncrossed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348590,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it turns out that we grid up the universe\nby the rate of ticking of the clocks of its travelers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355220,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if the universe has no clocks?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359550,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would be the case of a universe that\ncontained only light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363580,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light follows these tracks in between the\ntime grid, and never, ever cross contours"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369360,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that mark even a single tick of a clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371700,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For light, or any light-speed particle, the\nbeginning and end of every journey is the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377180,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both space and time lose meaning for a photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380250,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As Roger Penrose puts it: in order for time,\nand hence space to be meaningful, a universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384400,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "must be able to build a clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386720,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A clock must see the spacetime grid - and\nto do that it must travel at sub-light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392550,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in order to do that, the clock must have\nmass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396800,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you have even a single electron in the\nuniverse you can build a clock and can tell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401410,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the difference between the one light-second\nand the billion light-year sized universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407060,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with only light or other light-speed radiation\nthere’s nothing internal to those universes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412470,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can tell them apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re identical under the conformal transformation\nof rescaling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418440,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does this apply to our universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420690,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it may be that in the extreme far future\nour universe will contain only radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426870,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually all stars will die and their remnants\nwill decay - black holes will evaporate by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431310,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking radiation, and particles of matter\nwill decay into their lightest possible components."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436460,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the proton that’s speculative,\nbut it may be the case that we’re left with only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440490,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a universe of photons, electrons and positrons,\nand neutrinos, as well as gravitons - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445190,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum particles of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447260,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The photons and gravitons are massless - you\ncan’t build clocks with them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451720,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the others do have mass, so presumably\nthere’s still a way for the universe to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455620,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tell that it’s gigantic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458230,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose speculates that mass itself may not\nbe a fundamental property, and may eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463160,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay to leave massless electrons, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466320,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The standard model of particle physics predicts\neternal electrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470260,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s not absurd to imagine their mass\ndecaying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473080,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The masses of the elementary particles are\nnot some fundamental property of those particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476170,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- they come from the interactions of those\nparticles with quantum fields - the Higgs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479800,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field in the case of the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481280,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll come back to why we might expect the\nmass granted by the Higgs field to change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485350,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486350,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s the late universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488010,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Filled with only timeless radiation, it would\npossess no spacetime grid, so perhaps could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493050,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be considered sizeless."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495920,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about the early universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497740,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely it was full of particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499190,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well yeah, but those particles were effectively\nmassless also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504010,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two ways to think about this: A particle's\nenergy is a combination of its kinetic energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509130,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and rest mass energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511110,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kinetic energies were so high at the big bang\nthat rest mass energy was completely negligible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516250,
    "dDurationMs": 3569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- all particles behaved like light-speed particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519819,
    "dDurationMs": 4181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s precisely true for things like\nquarks and electrons, which gain their masses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524000,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from interactions with the Higgs field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526610,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that only works below a certain temperature\n- in the extreme temperatures of the Big Bang,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531470,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Higgs field could not grant mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533750,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a previous episode, obviously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537529,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, a change in the nature of the\nHiggs field - if it decayed to a lower energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541869,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- could eliminate elementary particle masses\nin the late universe also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545699,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the first tiny fraction of a second\nwe can think of the universe as being full"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549769,
    "dDurationMs": 3641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of effectively or actually massless particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553410,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hence the concept of time is as meaningless\nas in the late universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556990,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But does that really mean we can equate the\ntwo?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560410,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the black-tie formal answer we’d need\nto delve into the math of the conformal equivalence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566230,
    "dDurationMs": 2049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the beginning and end of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568279,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’ll just do it semi-formally without\nthe math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572649,
    "dDurationMs": 1781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe cocktail cyclic cosmology?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574430,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the other things Roger Penrose is famous\nfor is his Penrose diagrams - these are ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579410,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of mathematically transforming our grid of\nspacetime to fit infinite distance and time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584810,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the one map, while at the same time preserving\nthe 45 degree path of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588750,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The edges of this map represent “conformal\ninfinity” - where infinite space and time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594670,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are compressed onto an edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596540,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s for one dimension of space and one\ndimension of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600199,
    "dDurationMs": 6231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the full 4-D spacetime the edge becomes\na 3-D “hypersurface” in which infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606430,
    "dDurationMs": 6230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance and time are compressed or “conformally\nrescaled” into a finite space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612660,
    "dDurationMs": 6299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Similarly, the infinitesimal or “zero-sized”\npoint of the Big Bang can be rescaled into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618959,
    "dDurationMs": 1851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a finite space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620810,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was actually a discovery by one of Penrose’\ncolleagues, Paul Tod, and Tod’s work inspired"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626460,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose to follow this idea in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629589,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you stitch these rescaled “conformal\nhypersurfaces” together and you get this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634939,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "endless chain of universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637399,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose calls each universe in the chain an\n“aeon”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641959,
    "dDurationMs": 6011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, one important aspect of all of\nthis is that in order for the ends of time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647970,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be stitched together by this sort of conformal\nrescaling, the universe needs a positive cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654470,
    "dDurationMs": 1109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655579,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it needs dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657769,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which our universe has, so no problem, but\nit’s interesting that it worked out so neatly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663460,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose also says that CCC naturally gives\nyou dark matter - but we’ll skip that for now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670480,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only radiation - light and other massless\nparticles - can cross over this conformal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675300,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary from one aeon into the next."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678660,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because radiation can pass between universes,\nconformal cyclic cosmology gives a natural"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683110,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanation for the extreme smoothness that\nwe observe in the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688149,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was actually Penrose’s motivation in\nthe first place: to explain the apparent smoothness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693950,
    "dDurationMs": 1460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695410,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In particular, to explain its extremely low\nentropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699430,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If entropy can only rise over time, per the second law\nof thermodynamics, how did it get so low at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704089,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the start?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705089,
    "dDurationMs": 5511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is a standard explanation for the smoothness\nof the early universe - its cosmic inflation - a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710600,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "period of extreme exponential expansion that\nsmoothed things out in the first fraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715220,
    "dDurationMs": 1169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a second."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716389,
    "dDurationMs": 5001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Penrose insists that this does not explain\nthe low entropy of the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721390,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paul Tod’s conformal transformation of the\nBig Bang singularity helped Penrose to demonstrate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726899,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the smallness of the entropy at the Big\nBang is due to the tiny entropy in the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732089,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field at the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733910,
    "dDurationMs": 6119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That then inspired this daisy-chaining of\nuniverses, which eliminated the need for inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740029,
    "dDurationMs": 5441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In CCC, all of the energy - and, importantly,\nthe gravitational field - is smoothed out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745470,
    "dDurationMs": 3099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over infinite time between aeons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748569,
    "dDurationMs": 5471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inflation isn’t needed because the inflationary\nperiod is equivalent to the rescaled late-time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754040,
    "dDurationMs": 6029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forever of the previous universe, where exponential\nexpansion was fueled by dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760069,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the daisy-chain-verse to give you low\nentropy big bangs, you need to actually clean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765999,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entropy slate between aeons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768899,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To do that, black holes must swallow entropy\n- and destroy information."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774610,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is another issue with the CCC model - most\nphysicists think quantum information can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779300,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be destroyed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780600,
    "dDurationMs": 1609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Penrose isn’t so sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782209,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so how do you test an idea like this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786259,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wait infinite time and see if you find yourself\nin a big bang?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789639,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well actually, Penrose has proposed a test - and in fact conducted it and claimed convincing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795129,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796809,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If radiation can travel between universes,\nand if the end of the previous universe was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802800,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not completely smooth that could lead to features\nin the cosmic microwave background radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808269,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penroses proposes that the collisions of super\nmassive black holes in the previous universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813769,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may leave rings on the sky in the next."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817249,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in a paper with Vahe Gurzadyan he claims\ndetection of just such features."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823240,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Others have questioned the statistical methods\nof that study, which were non-standard, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828139,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they say there are no statistically significant\nfeatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832209,
    "dDurationMs": 4761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose and Gurzadyan have one more somewhat\nawesome speculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836970,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They wonder if civilizations might be able\nto communicate between universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842259,
    "dDurationMs": 7341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that, as well as photons, gravitational\nwaves should be able to pass between aeons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a super-duper-ridiculously advanced civilization\ncould manipulate the dances of gigantic black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855000,
    "dDurationMs": 4279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes, they could potentially send information\nbetween universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859279,
    "dDurationMs": 2161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far no evidence of that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861440,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wouldn’t it be cool if we found a message\nscrawled on the sky?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864569,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, hey guys, try not to mess up your aeon,\nremember to eat your greens, and try to have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869300,
    "dDurationMs": 5789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fun in this infinite chain of conformally\nrescaled spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875089,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments, I just want to\ntake a moment to thank everyone who supports"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878519,
    "dDurationMs": 1271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879790,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every little contribution is incredibly helpful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882579,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we normally list some of the Patreons supporters in the credits and the description, but we’ve just added"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887870,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new perk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889270,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All patreon supporters will have their names\nencoded in the orbital frequencies of colliding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894890,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppermassive black holes at the end of time\nto be propogated across the conformal infinity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899260,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the next Aeon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901060,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a special huge thank you to Caed Aldwych\nwho supports us at the Big Bang level, Caed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906819,
    "dDurationMs": 5041,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re inscribing your name on the Cosmic\nMicrowave Background of the next Universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911860,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to continue your glory and to seriously confuse\nthe astronomers of the next aeon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916980,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our recent episode we looked at the possibility\nthat viruses can travel between planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923579,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TLDR: it would be very very difficult for them to survive, and definitely the current virus didn’t come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930269,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from space ... but it’s not totally ruled\nout that maybe it once happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937129,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what you had to say."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940029,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Drakenkorin27, who is a bona fide virologist,\ngave us even more reason to doubt that any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944680,
    "dDurationMs": 2839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viruses from space have ever infected earth life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947519,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I had pointed out that alien viruses would\nneed to be DNA-based in order to infect DNA-based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953269,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Drakenkorin27 points out that the DNA of all life that evolved on earth uses the same code"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for instructing the molecular machinery to translate the DNA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These codes are made up of specific combinations of three DNA based pairs like ATG"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which everyone knows this is the beginning of a gene sequence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no reason to believe that alien\nDNA would have evolved exactly the same code"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980730,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alien viruses would have no way to deliver\ntheir demands - for example replicate me or to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986080,
    "dDurationMs": 1199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take me to your leader."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987279,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MC’s creates notes that if the RNA world\nhypothesis is correct, then viruses were the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993389,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first \"life\" to appear on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996649,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right - and that’s really what scientists\nmean when they say viruses may have predated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000769,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001769,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They mean that some virus-like entity - an\nRNA strand that could execute various simple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007319,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "functions - may have been in operation before\ncells evolved into modern viruses, rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013069,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than having sort of de-evolved from cells\nlater on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016639,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Devin Smith asks what protocols exist for\nprotecting earth from alien microbes, should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022589,
    "dDurationMs": 3451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they be discovered on a mission - say, to\nMars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026040,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So those protocols do exist - and basically\ninvolve extreme quarantine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031760,
    "dDurationMs": 7510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The outer space treaty demands all space samples\nbe quarantined in a biosafe level 3 facility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1039270,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Samples from somewhere like Mars that has\nthe potential for past habitability demand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043920,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a biosafe level 4 facility with additional\nsafeguards that haven’t actually been built"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049700,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050800,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if it was actually alien life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054350,
    "dDurationMs": 1750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Biosafe infinity seems prudent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056100,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not that it matters, we know one of the critters\nis going to sneak in by infecting one of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061250,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronauts and, like, controlling their mind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064680,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aurora Stark realizes we can have an asteroid\nimpact and alien virus wrapped up in one convenient"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073720,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single apocalypse, and asks that we stop giving\n2020 any more ideas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081360,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well sorry Aurora, but if 2020 watches spacetime\nthen in we’re trouble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085350,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I wonder, is it possible to be devoured by\na black hole while being blasted by a supernova,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090000,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and frozen by the heat death of the universe\nall at the same time?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
